00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:05.349 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  I have been since a couple of years very  much concerned with the state of our  planet and with the crisis that has been  captured by the term of the anthropocene  [Music]  my name is Jurgen Ren I'm director at  the Max Planck Institute for the history  of science here in Berlin and since a  couple of months I'm also a director at  the newly founded Max Planck Institute  for geoanthropology Indiana  the anthropocene is the proposed new  Epoch on of Earth history and the term  has been proposed by Paul cotson Nobel  Prize winner in chemistry director at  the Max Planck Institute for chemistry  in Mainz in the year 2000 the  anthropocene itself is a concept that  reaches much Beyond geology  it's the time of the human impact so  naturally you need many disciplines to  actually deal with this concept  humans are transforming geological time  into historical time by making use of of  fossil fuels for instance on the other  hand they have become themselves a  geological Force they have changed the  climate in a most dramatic way with  probably no analog in Earth history the  date that is proposed now for the  beginning of the anthropocene from the  stratigraphic point of view is just  after the first world war synchronous  was the first atomic explosion because  they live you know a radioactive  sediment all over the world  science is very good at drilling deep  holes and you know looking from a  specialized angle at problems and  understanding the mechanisms but the  anthropocene is a concept that requires  a different thinking and I think artists  are sometimes very good in capturing  sort of this holistic perspective  this is an interaction between science  and and art that you know requires from  the scientists an even more active you  know transgression of their laboratory  work into you know an exhibition that is  understandable to the public so let's  see what we can discover here  it is the research that actually Roots  the anthropocene in the sedimentary  record this is a peat core  and it shows clearly uh  signals of the nuclear weapon test so  you can really see some of the major  events marking the anthropocene you know  in those core elements  microplastics as you find it in river or  lake sediments are of course clearly a  product of of human activity and there  was one surprise in which this  microplastic was found in a layer I  think from the beginning of the 19th  century so people didn't yet produce  plastic but the micro the microplastic  is so  has such a tendency to spread that it  even spoils the sedimentary record and  goes into deeper layers you know which  of course obfuscates the the record but  it's still a sign of the profoundness  and of the uh the global impact that it  has  foreign  [Music]  we have quite a number of social  political activists so it's not just a  knowledge which comes from Academia and  it's being translated so to say in the  political field or in the social field  the point which became very relevant in  our Mississippi project was to see that  on the one side the anthropocene is a  planetary process on the other side it  articulates itself in very specific  historical contexts and one has to have  developed a sensibility to to deal with  the anthropocynical  repercussions in these kind of concrete  social contexts we were interacting with  local activists and not just of course  you know bringing them some academic  insights but learning from them about  sort of the local consequences and the  local actions they are taking so the  anthropocene discourse is really about  knowledge in action  foreign  [Music]  so when people ask about which direction  which overall direction we have to take  in order to cope with the challenges of  the anthropocene  there are basically two answers one is  Back To Nature and the other answer is a  modernist answer let's you know govern  nature even more let's promote  technologies that you know increase the  control of humanity over nature  I personally think both will not work  because we have so deeply interfered  with the planetary system that's the  meaning of the anthropocene that there  is no way back to Nature if we continue  to interfere in the way we have done so  in the past  this will lead to collapse we have to  stop for sure the use of fossil fuels so  we have to rethink our entire Energy  System and not just naturally but on a  global level  it's very important that scientists from  all over the world meet and discuss  these problems and they should do it in  an open way interacting also with a  larger public so I think the Berlin  science week is a great event in which  science is again center stage in a way  but has also to confront its  responsibility for dealing with the  challenges of this world it needs to be  abroad a global and a public discussion  so I very much cherished these events  even though you know they require a lot  of traveling which is you know  increasing our carbon footprint but some  of these discussions actually need  personal encounters in particular for  the younger people they need to have the  opportunity to network to meet people  from other parts of the world and talk  about not just their Specialties but the  kind of global problems that are  connected to the anthropocene  we know enough to act I mean there's no  reason that we have to wait for further  knowledge about you know climate change  to act upon it you know it's absolutely  necessary to act quickly and to act  thoroughly and with to concentrate all  our energies to prevent uh you know this  Global Climate crisis  [Music]

